"""Extra practical sections for batch 28 Search Console guides (general advice; Google facts stay on the main pages)."""

MORE = {
"google-search-console-performance-report": ("A monthly review in fifteen minutes", "<p>Set the date range to the last three months and compare it with the previous period. Note total clicks and impressions first, then switch to the Queries tab and sort by impressions to find the searches you appear for most. Look for three kinds of opportunity: queries for services you offer where your position is between about 5 and 20, queries with many impressions but a low click-through rate, and queries for services you don't have a page for yet. Then open the Pages tab to see which URLs earn those impressions. Pick one page to improve each month: tighten the title so it matches the search, answer the question in the first paragraph, add the details customers ask about and link to it from related pages. Write down what you changed and when, so next month's review can tell you whether it worked. For seasonal businesses in Orange County, such as HVAC, pool services or tax preparers, compare the same months year over year as well, so a seasonal dip isn't mistaken for a problem.</p>"),
"google-search-console-url-inspection": ("A checklist after publishing a new page", "<p>Every time you publish an important page, such as a new service, location or offer, run the same short routine. First, make sure the page is linked from at least two relevant pages on your site, such as the homepage or main services page, so Google can find it without relying on a request. Second, confirm it is in your sitemap if your site uses one. Third, inspect the URL and run a live test to confirm Google can fetch the page and that nothing blocks it, such as a noindex tag left over from a staging site or a robots.txt rule. Fourth, view the tested page screenshot to check the main content is visible. Fifth, request indexing once. Then leave it alone for a week or two before inspecting again. If the page is still not indexed, compare it with similar pages that are: is it clearly different and useful, or does it repeat what another page says? Thin or near-duplicate pages are often crawled but not indexed, so the fix is usually better content rather than more requests.</p>"),
"google-search-console-page-indexing-report": ("How we triage indexing for small business sites", "<p>When a business in Orange County tells us pages are missing from Google, we work through the same order. First, we search site: for the domain and for the most important URLs, because that answers the question for most small sites in minutes. Second, we inspect the homepage and key service pages with URL Inspection to see their exact status and Google-selected canonical. Third, only if several important pages are missing do we open the Page indexing report and group the unindexed URLs by reason. Fourth, we decide for each reason whether it is intended, such as tag archives, search pages or old URLs that redirect, or a real problem, such as an accidental noindex or a page that duplicates another. Fifth, we fix the real problems, strengthen internal links and make sure the pages are worth indexing. Most of the time, the cause is simple: new pages with no links pointing to them, pages that are too similar to each other, or settings left over from development.</p>"),
"google-search-console-sitemaps-report": ("What a clean sitemap looks like", "<p>A clean sitemap lists only the pages you want people to find in Google: the homepage, service and location pages, important blog posts and key information pages. It leaves out pages that redirect, return errors, carry a noindex tag or exist only for internal use, such as cart, account, thank-you or search result pages. Every URL in it should use the same version of your domain that your site actually uses, typically https and either www or non-www consistently. If your sitemap is generated by a plugin, check its settings once: many plugins include tag archives, author pages or media attachment pages by default, which rarely help a small business. After any redesign or migration, open the sitemap in a browser, spot-check a dozen URLs to confirm they load and match the live site, and resubmit it. A sitemap that matches your real, indexable pages makes the Sitemaps and Page indexing reports much easier to read, because the numbers then describe pages you care about.</p>"),
"google-search-console-links-report": ("Local link ideas that fit a small business", "<p>The most useful links for a local business usually come from relationships it already has. Start with organizations you belong to: chambers of commerce, trade associations and business improvement districts often have member directories. Then think about the people you work with: suppliers, manufacturers whose products you install, venues you work at and complementary businesses that refer customers to you. Community involvement can earn links naturally when you sponsor a youth team, support a charity event or host a workshop that a local organization lists. Useful local content can also attract links, such as a guide to permits for a type of project in your city or a seasonal checklist that local news or community sites want to share. Avoid shortcuts such as paid link packages, link exchanges at scale or low-quality directories; they go against Google's spam policies and can lead to a manual action. A handful of genuine, relevant local links is worth more than hundreds of irrelevant ones. Check the report again a few months later to see whether those relationships show up as linking sites.</p>"),
"google-search-console-manual-actions": ("Keeping records that make a review easier", "<p>If you ever need to send a reconsideration request, good records make it faster and more convincing. Keep a simple log of the SEO and marketing work done on your site: who did it, when and what it involved, including any link building, guest posting or content production. Keep copies of agency reports and invoices. If you receive a manual action, add to the log each step you take: the pages or links you found, what you changed or removed, which site owners you contacted and when, and what remains. Google says a good request explains the exact issue, the steps taken and the outcome; a log lets you write that clearly instead of from memory. It also protects the business when people change: a new marketing manager or agency can see what happened before and avoid repeating it. For businesses in Anaheim and across Orange County that have worked with several providers over the years, this record is often the missing piece when a problem surfaces.</p>"),
"google-search-console-security-issues": ("A simple incident plan", "<p>Agree in advance who does what if your website is hacked, so a problem is handled in hours rather than weeks. Write down who has access to the hosting account, domain registrar, WordPress admin and Search Console, and keep that list somewhere safe outside the website. Know how to contact your host's support and whether your plan includes malware cleanup. Make sure backups run automatically and that you know how to restore one, ideally having tested it once. Decide who will check whether customer information, such as contact form submissions, could have been exposed and who will tell customers if so. When an issue appears, take a copy of the site for investigation, clean it or restore a known clean backup, patch the vulnerability, change every password, then check Search Console and request review. Afterwards, note what happened and what you changed. A plan like this is a single page of notes, but it turns a stressful emergency into a routine task.</p>"),
"google-search-console-users-and-permissions": ("A quarterly access check", "<p>Put a short access review in the calendar every three months. Open Settings, then Users and permissions, and read down the list. For each person, ask whether they still work with the business and whether they need their current level of access. Remove anyone who has left and lower access for people who only read reports. Check that at least one verified owner is an account controlled by the business, not by an individual or an outside provider, and that you know how that verification works, for example the DNS record or HTML file that proves it. If your site is being rebuilt, warn the developer not to remove verification files or tags without checking. Do the same review for related tools at the same time, including your Google Business Profile, Google Analytics, your website admin and your domain registrar, so access stays consistent. Keep a short written record of who has which role and why, and update it whenever you change access. If you work with an agency, put in the contract that the business remains the verified owner and that agency access ends when the work ends. Ten minutes a quarter prevents the most common problem we see with Orange County businesses: important data and settings controlled by someone who has moved on.</p>"),
"google-search-console-removals-tool": ("Outdated information on other websites", "<p>Many business owners find outdated details about their business on sites they don't control, such as an old address on a directory, a former owner's phone number or an old menu. The Removals tool can't change those pages, because it only covers sites you own. The practical approach is to contact each site owner or use the platform's own correction process, such as claiming the listing on a directory and updating it. If the information has already been removed from the page but still appears in Google's results, Google provides a separate way to request a refresh of outdated content. Meanwhile, make sure your own website, Google Business Profile and main directory listings all show the current, consistent details, so search engines and AI assistants have a clear source of truth. For businesses that have moved within Orange County or changed phone numbers, this cleanup often matters more for customers than anything in the Removals tool.</p>"),
}


def add_more(pages):
    for p in pages:
        if p["slug"] in MORE:
            h, b = MORE[p["slug"]]
            p["sections"].insert(len(p["sections"]) - 1, {"heading": h, "body_html": b})
